#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175762 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e22ff208-ff10-5106-8922-26adc9007e40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The First Column Case"}] :question/id #uuid "53bf6a59-06ab-5597-999c-20f7ca55bba2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9088" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/8a5b765f60afdb05946a2ed393c2776c532fca4c2905969928b8dc66b55d8913.png)\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = P J P^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]-(4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=-x_2.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/ca51c47bc0e065fa8ec1303f235f5b5e0c818bf271abbfb90ba96219fd6dd91a.png)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/34e1d2e3dcdfd3c46c3e2ca5360fd596e307efe042f6676413736a100d058155.png)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{-1}{1} = -1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175763 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac67bb72-7802-547d-b9d9-ab238cc3768f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The Second Column Case"}] :question/id #uuid "90e87dca-f423-5587-b39a-5d6e931db18e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9089" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/35e38dfcccab52240fa316b41acacf74548e7218a5d79d856f69c947f3d26394.png)\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = P J P^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/9bff79e13c06645253e57b8e6f43689c7ce21ac818c3ffdc677e03b5a9b08eb7.png) of the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ such that $(A-4I)\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{v}_1.$\n\nNow, computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A - 4I)\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{v}_1$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=-\\dfrac 15 - x_2.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/44f1c49ffc09942f7062c8e4da4419a214551cdf659ee92be03556c52a2f3005.png)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=0,$ we get  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/e2ad676d41fff87d0c519bf6cbb369a5ec9ac209c8b2b9353a658d9307d6109d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175785 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53e40a15-120c-5587-979b-cc1369d244bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Columns of a Transformation Matrix in a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "b4a6d165-9c52-59e6-af4b-c5777066e877" :question/math-academy-id "e-9227" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/54ae1bc6614a1fe70fac039fbdd3defd2aeeceb3648679ee3d4c9ad10f6f15b5.png)\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = P J P^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/a985e5861e7360654b98ca334fac6d55f435e271dcc52b313e350f74a4c755c5.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J = J_2(1).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda=1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $\\mathbf x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/624e955f479c8193ff32f3bebf0decb7b1d6b45fb5ec44db637d956e3a8d1ea7.png)\nand\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/0855b40d83950bcdfeda8447e74187f498fbfe73050e1e8fd7755a1ad4b1dbd0.png)\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac67bb72-7802-547d-b9d9-ab238cc3768f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The Second Column Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce145732-4daa-5c68-bec0-1e7ba678cec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173551} {:answer/id #uuid "30b95d73-cd39-51b6-9de2-5af01b1c7e04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173552} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6a0bf5-cf26-5b45-857d-aba7a4799609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173553} {:answer/id #uuid "4c06d87b-96a2-5f68-a7a1-243ae1f9a5e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173554} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd835e4-ef02-51c1-83f4-566583bb909b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c06d87b-96a2-5f68-a7a1-243ae1f9a5e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173554} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d153c99-a625-5397-b0e6-129ef4af5298" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b0fdb675-21a9-5763-9fe7-cb59859241e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-108728" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a-2b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-3I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]-(3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-3I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-1+2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-2b=1-2\\cdot 1=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e22ff208-ff10-5106-8922-26adc9007e40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The First Column Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dede4b19-3ef0-5d7d-9a10-2719ca3ad3d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368411} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1f13dd-3c56-5f94-b8bd-fe659506da1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368412} {:answer/id #uuid "29260b16-7dda-53f3-aa36-3a1a224eaac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368413} {:answer/id #uuid "3d90eca7-10e1-50d5-b71a-c7a81cba4df1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368414} {:answer/id #uuid "f917e3d2-3932-5cbe-8c55-1d21cb930d23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29260b16-7dda-53f3-aa36-3a1a224eaac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368413} :answer-field/id #uuid "61536069-9ccd-5c62-9c10-8b2cda46913e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b3e3ac65-7969-52f3-8813-91cf6542014e" :question/math-academy-id "q-109364" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-(2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368416 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e22ff208-ff10-5106-8922-26adc9007e40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The First Column Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "745b899b-6f8c-5a3b-939e-73db68e01a68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368418} {:answer/id #uuid "b95d7f97-82d6-55c5-9499-4585df72a04b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368419} {:answer/id #uuid "c4629be4-9dfe-588d-b298-8ecc7c8275c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368420} {:answer/id #uuid "7142b358-b0c1-5615-b109-d36e7b279faf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368421} {:answer/id #uuid "6138f9b7-dc45-5f06-b2ba-868ce10dd0da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b95d7f97-82d6-55c5-9499-4585df72a04b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368419} :answer-field/id #uuid "708d062f-c5a2-5443-99b1-be0b4624fc2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9448529-9efa-52b6-b130-0c79264ced81" :question/math-academy-id "q-109365" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e22ff208-ff10-5106-8922-26adc9007e40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The First Column Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acc54f4b-4cbe-5a60-868d-e67f9437060d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368425} {:answer/id #uuid "011f06ee-3eae-5315-98ef-d433ffc7c210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368426} {:answer/id #uuid "5c784600-dcae-5070-b256-79c059a053ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368427} {:answer/id #uuid "260c97e7-3880-52ef-afae-c5bc38a8d09d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368428} {:answer/id #uuid "9320118f-1026-58ff-bab3-ca39a49583e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "260c97e7-3880-52ef-afae-c5bc38a8d09d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368428} :answer-field/id #uuid "2049ce51-4b5a-5774-a5e7-c80fd437951f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7c9362e-1f6d-5912-a84b-409dc6dd95b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-109366" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-(4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{1}=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e22ff208-ff10-5106-8922-26adc9007e40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The First Column Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7b3a9e2-58e8-5498-b3ad-3245b717574f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173599} {:answer/id #uuid "0c3c84ac-e05e-53fb-91e4-46f3a27e6997" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173600} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb202cb-9a10-553f-8be3-a81f2e4cbd45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173601} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8eb27b-da2a-569a-9358-53ea0291922b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173602} {:answer/id #uuid "6d540e91-3815-51d8-87f0-02a314bf2df1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7b3a9e2-58e8-5498-b3ad-3245b717574f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173599} :answer-field/id #uuid "195417ba-da51-500f-88d3-50d8726a4ddc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "addb688e-b364-54ad-8e80-207a23ed2756" :question/math-academy-id "q-109368" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=7.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-7I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]-(7)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1}{-2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e22ff208-ff10-5106-8922-26adc9007e40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The First Column Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec6b6a57-fc11-53c0-956c-973499e246dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368432} {:answer/id #uuid "4e1823cc-35db-5636-9863-344175ef9197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368433} {:answer/id #uuid "0be79aa7-58e6-520b-bdf1-61b8420aba74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368434} {:answer/id #uuid "b5115f9e-3bdd-591c-8227-0ef7f6a53d8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368435} {:answer/id #uuid "9dabfb01-866f-5e0e-902f-316058a7a2e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec6b6a57-fc11-53c0-956c-973499e246dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368432} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee6ebc32-9ea0-5be4-81b3-4895fabc152c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5cd83803-71ea-5ffc-92fb-6655ede9cef2" :question/math-academy-id "q-109369" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=7.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-7I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]-(7)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac67bb72-7802-547d-b9d9-ab238cc3768f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The Second Column Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d856a76a-189c-53cb-8451-376c349c6212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368439} {:answer/id #uuid "c29d4581-842d-5248-9c8d-5ef4910e8fd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368440} {:answer/id #uuid "5da28da4-1950-5635-a3f6-d5ab1b381d3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368441} {:answer/id #uuid "6a17e738-8dc5-527e-8522-7b5977c87f9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368442} {:answer/id #uuid "d70f4ec1-792d-56ae-8e5d-ac700dc445a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a17e738-8dc5-527e-8522-7b5977c87f9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368442} :answer-field/id #uuid "98c22df6-b215-52e9-b7ba-81842adec705" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ecea06a6-536c-5355-97c9-b5f5adf55204" :question/math-academy-id "q-109373" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $2a+2b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-2I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]-(2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-2I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $2a+2b=2\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2})+2\\cdot 1=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac67bb72-7802-547d-b9d9-ab238cc3768f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The Second Column Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b0b4bda-442e-56bd-8d5a-e2f69556dd57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368446} {:answer/id #uuid "5696bc5d-15b0-5866-924d-435487a51556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368447} {:answer/id #uuid "e6488c56-66dc-53ee-8ad8-83b58ee34db0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368448} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b5ee30-714a-58e3-b074-2b8a7b43b204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368449} {:answer/id #uuid "8dd42835-c342-5db9-8832-f75d634fb5d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5696bc5d-15b0-5866-924d-435487a51556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368447} :answer-field/id #uuid "3069200a-1058-5355-9027-104699ae0fcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9cb0040f-1901-5364-98db-76a7b1845057" :question/math-academy-id "q-109377" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & a \\\\ 2 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $6a-3b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-(-3)I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-(-3)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-(-3)I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $6a-3b=6\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}-3\\cdot 2=-\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac67bb72-7802-547d-b9d9-ab238cc3768f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The Second Column Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a79a0082-1dff-588d-bd51-b7881690182a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173605} {:answer/id #uuid "db4da122-d18d-57f4-af85-19d8adfa4b83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173606} {:answer/id #uuid "dd840fc7-d8ad-5a86-b676-ed88f9534b30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173607} {:answer/id #uuid "e63a0c7b-91d2-5beb-8fb6-3939ac0e4efd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173608} {:answer/id #uuid "645af67c-6061-5b15-bdc4-67c1efe66121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db4da122-d18d-57f4-af85-19d8adfa4b83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173606} :answer-field/id #uuid "8470e3e6-af6c-5e75-9875-64214dc459e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "71955644-7fce-58e7-aa23-c8e43615d89a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109381" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a-2b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=1+2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-2b=3-2\\cdot 1=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac67bb72-7802-547d-b9d9-ab238cc3768f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The Second Column Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01bb4e0a-f2e7-5426-91e8-979779148164" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368453} {:answer/id #uuid "71e5c32f-b67d-5a19-87c5-102cf8097ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368454} {:answer/id #uuid "5ef9b9c1-faa4-595b-98c1-03c339aa6df6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368455} {:answer/id #uuid "6c5dc5b8-3839-50d7-bd63-601bd9426d12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368456} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d673c1-455c-5466-a5b7-026527c1f826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71e5c32f-b67d-5a19-87c5-102cf8097ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368454} :answer-field/id #uuid "85d4ddb2-8ebe-5e50-bddf-837db6b0de9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "285c9388-32dd-5ba5-be11-07c4640c0583" :question/math-academy-id "q-109384" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a-2b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=7.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-7I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-7I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]-(7)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-7I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-2b=\\frac{5}{2}-2\\cdot 1=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53e40a15-120c-5587-979b-cc1369d244bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Columns of a Transformation Matrix in a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "862dd746-e765-5276-9c3d-a3df22502c34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173875} {:answer/id #uuid "96dc658c-f069-51f0-ab2d-d1edef81492e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173876} {:answer/id #uuid "a76729e7-40ac-5002-883b-ca750e2e549d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173877} {:answer/id #uuid "d95e6af2-6a09-532e-b47a-027634bf7fca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173878} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4fcc32-97fa-52dd-a9a0-5800b1d12609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "862dd746-e765-5276-9c3d-a3df22502c34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173875} :answer-field/id #uuid "31b89bfc-b72f-5819-96e0-040551b0f540" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e0868ec-2423-5a95-9212-2ae581f51f78" :question/math-academy-id "q-112432" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(0).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(0) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-0I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $x$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-0I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53e40a15-120c-5587-979b-cc1369d244bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Columns of a Transformation Matrix in a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85565018-31d1-57d9-92c1-d182079cba5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368460} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b1457e-fddc-566d-a293-c351736af50d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368461} {:answer/id #uuid "fee9e320-b59a-5fd0-97be-600ea3b41aa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368462} {:answer/id #uuid "1e4f7ad2-71a3-5d79-81e0-2019f0510f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368463} {:answer/id #uuid "2aac1bab-e9e0-5bb2-b1ee-6137aa5ccdaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8b1457e-fddc-566d-a293-c351736af50d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368461} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2fe38d2-9500-51a9-a6b5-b91c0eb0adad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "677047c1-d29d-502f-b1c8-b8ffeadb43fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-112497" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(2).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(2) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-2I)y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=z,\n$$\n\nand, therefore,\n\n$$\n(A-2I)^{2}y=(A-2I)z=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53e40a15-120c-5587-979b-cc1369d244bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Columns of a Transformation Matrix in a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26b2a9d4-763c-5fbe-b791-61c0844ef508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368467} {:answer/id #uuid "9b67c429-5fa5-535c-b4e0-e97398296d7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368468} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb66f5e-96ff-5a0a-8687-6f288ff83890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368469} {:answer/id #uuid "2922b2fc-80b0-5558-8548-d5390edc826e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368470} {:answer/id #uuid "369536e4-b743-569b-88ab-787e085fb523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "369536e4-b743-569b-88ab-787e085fb523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368471} :answer-field/id #uuid "50d31c41-dc21-5c84-ad91-389e409c2a82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6742def-6999-5043-9553-f817f1ed7b14" :question/math-academy-id "q-112500" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(-2).$  \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-2) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAmong the given vectors, only $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-(-2)I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=y,\n$$\n\nand, therefore,\n\n$$\n(A-(-2)I)^{2}x=(A+2I)y=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53e40a15-120c-5587-979b-cc1369d244bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Columns of a Transformation Matrix in a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "917693b9-203c-5b79-a7f0-99137edc04ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368474} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f57904-5515-5ad9-ac36-7f8790d29b07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368475} {:answer/id #uuid "6d03a5fb-8872-526a-a44e-fa859664a764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368476} {:answer/id #uuid "0b843757-d1f5-58ef-a45b-0060a7576173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368477} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c7706e-6d12-5bb9-9de4-6c773f9cfc90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ and $x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "917693b9-203c-5b79-a7f0-99137edc04ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368474} :answer-field/id #uuid "8729ed1f-47c2-52e8-be92-290b0a751f0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00c0b9a4-6019-5f40-87c1-5d44133b3318" :question/math-academy-id "q-112505" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & -25 \\\\ 9 & 14\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(-1).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-(-1)I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-(-1)I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =-1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & -25 \\\\ 9 & 14\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & -25 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $z$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-(-1)I)z=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & -25 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-(-1)I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53e40a15-120c-5587-979b-cc1369d244bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Columns of a Transformation Matrix in a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e969f64-36d1-5a88-86d1-0b855168a33b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368481} {:answer/id #uuid "001d4efa-734f-5ec9-ab6d-d4e7e7a0e486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and  $z$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368482} {:answer/id #uuid "0e7ef2b8-ba1a-5c3c-94f9-3caa9b165ff7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ and  $z$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368483} {:answer/id #uuid "b11e3209-a888-55b6-821d-fbfb2615b428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368484} {:answer/id #uuid "9690cda4-e42a-5466-b1ca-eb2981e18e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b11e3209-a888-55b6-821d-fbfb2615b428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368484} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f8a44c5-b0fd-5a34-95e3-676c8f262da6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c78f3931-0d81-5d94-bb0e-66706a1ecbb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-112506" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(1).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $y$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-1I)y=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."}]}
